# 0001-limiter-window.patch — rate limiter: fixed window, atomic increment,
# honest Retry-After.
#
# Files: src/interfaces/middleware/rate_limit.rs
#        src/interfaces/api/handlers/grant_handler.rs            (call site)
#        src/application/services/recipient_notification_service.rs (call site)
# Applies to: a clean tree at HEAD (389e161b). Standalone: independent of
#             0002/0003/0004, which touch disjoint files.
# Policy flag: none. This is a correctness fix; no operator decision needed.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES (behaviour)
#   1. The window no longer slides. One convention, documented in the module
#      header: a key's window opens on its FIRST request in it (count + start
#      instant in one moka value) and closes window_secs later; every request
#      inside it — allowed or rejected — increments the count; the next request
#      after it closes opens a fresh window. Before: `get(ip).unwrap_or(0) + 1`
#      then `insert(...)`, and moka resets a key's TTL on every write, so each
#      rejected retry pushed the expiry out and a client could hold its own
#      lockout open indefinitely.
#   2. `Retry-After` (and the 429 body's `retry_after_secs`) now report the time
#      left in that key's window, rounded up and floored at 1 s, via the new
#      `RateLimiter::retry_after_for(key)`. Before: `retry_after()` returned the
#      configured window as a constant, so a client that had already spent 59 of
#      60 s was told to wait 60 more. The ceiling is hand-rolled
#      (`as_secs() + u64::from(subsec_nanos() > 0)`) because `Duration::div_ceil`
#      is NOT stable std — rustc 1.99.0 answers E0599 — and the code comment says
#      so, so nobody "simplifies" it back into a non-compiling call.
#   3. The increment is atomic: one `cache.entry_by_ref(key).and_upsert_with(..)`
#      that runs under moka's per-key lock and receives the current value, so N
#      concurrent requests on one key hand out counts 1..=N and exactly
#      min(N, max_requests) are allowed. Before: a read-then-write pair with no
#      mutual exclusion, so threads that read between a peer's `get` and its
#      `insert` all incremented the same stale count (a burst against a budget
#      of 1 could be served by many threads). Different keys take different
#      locks and still proceed in parallel — per-key budgets stay independent.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY KEEPS
#   * Signatures: `RateLimiter::new(max, window_secs, max_entries)`,
#     `check_and_increment(&str) -> Result<u32, ()>` (still a unit error, so
#     every existing `.is_ok()` / `Err(())` caller and test is untouched).
#   * Zero-arg `retry_after() -> u64` stays, now documented as the CEILING any
#     key's wait can be (it is still `window_secs`, which no window can exceed).
#     It is kept because callers and the RED test corpus must compile against
#     the pre-patch surface too; every site that has a key uses the new
#     `retry_after_for(key)`: the middleware (`enforce`) and the two non-
#     middleware 429s that build their own body/header
#     (`grant_handler::create_grant`'s email-invite branch and
#     `recipient_notification_service`'s per-recipient limiter).
#   * `too_many_requests(retry_after)` wire shape: 429 + `Retry-After` header +
#     `{"error":"Too many requests","retry_after_secs":N}`.
#   * A handler-level rejection (400) still spends budget: the limiter runs
#     before `next.run`.
#   * `benches/ROUND11.md` and its "`and_upsert_with` benchmarked slower still"
#     note are left alone (historical record); the code comment that repeated
#     that reasoning is gone, since the locked upsert is now required for
#     correctness rather than a performance choice.
#
# TESTS THAT PIN IT (all in rate_limit.rs #[cfg(test)])
#
#   RED on v0.10.0 / GREEN after this patch — the only two tests in this patch
#   whose pass/fail differs between the trees:
#   * rejected_requests_do_not_extend_the_window — RED on v0.10.0 (the request
#     at t≈5 s is still rejected there because the rejected attempt at t≈2 s
#     refreshed the TTL to t≈6 s), GREEN here.
#   * retry_after_header_shrinks_as_the_window_elapses — RED on v0.10.0 (both
#     429s 3 s apart report the constant 6), GREEN here; also asserts the body
#     field equals the header and that the zero-arg ceiling is the window.
#
#   GUARDS — invariants kept so a regression cannot slip back in. They are NOT
#   counted as RED/GREEN evidence in this series and must never appear in the RED
#   column: where a guard's server-level behaviour is identical on both trees
#   (measured below), its pass/fail carries no information about the fix:
#   * concurrent_burst_admits_exactly_one_request — 64 threads released from a
#     barrier against a budget of 1 must serve exactly one, and a second key must
#     still be served (per-key serialisation, not a global lock). Server-level
#     bursts are IDENTICAL on both builds, so this test is not in the RED column:
#     the build side drove the same 64-request burst at the real server 5x per
#     build and got 1 served / 63 rejected every time (docs/re/evidence/
#     c0-burst.tsv unpatched, c1-burst.tsv patched; corpus cases
#     `concurrent-burst-rejects-8` / `-16`, docs/re/evidence/A-cases.md).
#
#     Reconciles with the build side's 3 clean runs of this test on the v0.10.0
#     arm: the pre-patch shape does overshoot, but only sometimes and only under
#     a wide enough arrival spread. Measured on a standalone instrument with both
#     bodies verbatim and the lockfile's moka, 10 trials per configuration, two
#     independent runs: overshoot trials at budget 1 were 6/10, 3/10, 0/10
#     (opt 0/1/3) in one run and 1/10, 3/10, 6/10 in the other, with ambient load
#     differing between them. At budget 8: 7/10, 3/10, 1/10 and 8/10, 1/10, 3/10.
#     The harness iterates four configurations per invocation (patched/original x
#     budget 1/8) and the command runs three opt-levels, i.e. 12 configurations x
#     10 trials per run, SIX of them patched (12 patched configurations across
#     runs A+B). The upsert shape served EXACTLY its budget in all of them
#     (0 overshoot trials). So this test is a
#     FLAKY detector, and its flakiness is the finding: same opt-level, opposite
#     ordering between the two runs.
#
#     The non-atomicity itself is not stochastic: interleaving the two halves of
#     the old read/write pair by hand (instrument, `deterministic` mode) serves 2
#     on a budget of 1 at EVERY opt-level, while the upsert shape answers
#     Ok(1) then Err(()) for the same two requests. That is what this patch
#     fixes, and no arrival spread can change it.
#     (docs/re/patches/atomicity-guard-standalone-run.txt, instrument
#     atomicity-guard-harness.rs + atomicity-guard-harness-Cargo.toml.)
#
#   * two_routes_share_one_budget — guards the per-instance accounting (one Arc
#     = one budget) and the 429 shape through the rewrite; true on both trees.
#     Its wiring claim is about the state of main.rs at HEAD, and 0002 repoints
#     this test because it changes that wiring.
#
#   Every test above uses only pre-existing public symbols (`RateLimiter::new`,
#   `check_and_increment`, zero-arg `retry_after`, the `rate_limit_*` layers,
#   `too_many_requests`) so the module can be copied verbatim onto v0.10.0 for
#   the RED run.
